{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And you shall guard yourself from every evil thing</b> — that the Satan prosecutes at a time of danger; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:10). Now, what seems correct in my eyes regarding the matter of this commandment is that Scripture warns at a time when sin is readily found. For it is well known from the practices of military camps that go forth to war that they will eat every abomination, steal and extort, and will not refrain even from adultery and every act of villainy — for the straightforward among human beings by nature don cruelty and wrath when a camp goes out against an enemy. Therefore Scripture warned: <b>and you shall guard yourself from every evil thing</b>. And according to the plain sense, this is a warning against all that is forbidden. And in the Sifrei (Ki Teitzei 119): \"<b>And you shall guard yourself from every evil thing</b> — do I perhaps hear [that it concerns] impurities and purities and tithes? Scripture says: <b>ervah</b> ('nakedness,' i.e., sexual immorality). I have only ervah; from where do I include idolatry, forbidden sexual relations, bloodshed, and blasphemy? Scripture says: <b>and you shall guard yourself from every evil thing.</b> Perhaps [it concerns] impurities and purities and tithes? Scripture says: <b>ervah</b> — just as ervah is a specific act for which the Canaanites were driven out and which removes the Shekhinah, so too every act for which the Canaanites were driven out and which removes the Shekhinah. When it says <b>davar</b> ('thing'), it includes even lashon ha-ra (evil speech).\" This too is for the reason we have explained: for besides the warnings that already exist for these grave transgressions, it adds a prohibition in the camp, to guard against all these transgressions, so that the Shekhinah not withdraw from Israel who are there, as it says, \"for HaShem your Elohim walks in the midst of your camp.\" And one who commits the great transgressions in the camp is like those of whom it is written (Jeremiah 7:30), \"they have set their abominations in the house which is called by My name, to defile it\" — and furthermore, that the enemies not gain power over us if we do the very deeds that caused them [the Canaanites] to be driven out before us, and this is the meaning of \"and to deliver your enemies before you.\" And they added lashon ha-ra in it, so that they not multiply disputes among themselves, and so that they not inflict upon themselves a blow far greater than [what] the enemies [could inflict]."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Sifrei citation's lemma reads 'קיט' (119) in the standard numbering of Sifrei Devaronomy, section Ki Teitzei; I have preserved this as given in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:54:31.247767058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}